**Why a clean interface is useful?**

> **It makes components interchangeable, reducing future maintenance costs.**

When a piece of software exposes a simple, well‑defined interface, you can swap out its implementation without touching the code that uses it. This decoupling means you can patch bugs, improve performance, or switch to a completely different underlying library, all while keeping downstream code unchanged.

**Example 006 – A Payment Gateway Wrapper**

```text
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void charge(double amount, String currency, CreditCard card);
}
```

In a clean‑interface scenario, the rest of the application only knows it has a `PaymentProcessor`. Today we use Stripe, tomorrow we might switch to Adyen. Because the interface remains the same, the calling code stays untouched; only the concrete class that implements `PaymentProcessor` changes. This keeps the system flexible and lowers the risk of ripple‑effects during upgrades.

**Why a clean interface is useful**

A clean interface allows developers to *focus on what a component does, not how it does it*.  
When an interface exposes only the essential operations, the rest of the code can treat the component as an **abstraction**. This makes it easier to swap implementations, test in isolation, and reason about the system’s behavior.

*Example 009 – User Authentication Module*

```java
// Clean interface
public interface AuthService {
    boolean authenticate(String username, String password);
    void logout(String sessionId);
}
```

*Why it matters:*  
The rest of the application only needs to know that credentials can be verified and sessions can be terminated. It never cares whether the underlying authentication uses a JDBC database, an LDAP server, or an external OAuth provider. Because the interface is clean, the `AuthService` implementation can be swapped, mocked for tests, or extended without touching any code that depends on it.

A **clean interface** is useful because it lets developers use a component without having to understand all of its internal workings.  
**One reason:** it reduces cognitive load and makes the component easier to adopt, test, maintain, and replace.

